Listing 17-29: Defining timeout with race and sleep
If the future_to_try succeeds and we get a Left(output) , we return Ok(output) . If the
sleep timer elapses instead and we get a Right(()) , we ignore the () with _ and return
Err(max_time) instead.
With that, we have a working timeout built out of two other async helpers. If we run our code,
it will print the failure mode after the timeout:
Because futures compose with other futures, you can build really powerful tools using smaller
async building blocks. For example, you can use this same approach to combine timeouts with
retries, and in turn use those with operations such as network calls (one of the examples from
the beginning of the chapter).
In practice, you’ll usually work directly with async and await , and secondarily with functions
and macros such as join , join_all , race , and so on. You’ll only need to reach for pin now
and again to use futures with those APIs.
